# Authentication

<p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">{formattedDate}</p>

There is no reason for a service built today to authenticate anyone by email.

For the service it is a product of its own. Sending mail means infrastructure, rendered templates, and the copy inside every message. Password recovery, one-time codes, changing the address — all of it has to be built, and none of it exists until an address is asked for.

A passkey needs none of that. The credential is already in the browser. Implementing one is incomparably simpler and cheaper.

It is no better for the person signing up. Confirmation means opening a mailbox and waiting for the letter. The password that comes with the address is unsafe either way. People who know it reuse it across services. People who do not know it leave it to a password manager, and then it is no different from a passkey — only a strange, unsafe, inconvenient copy.

## The truth

Services accept the inconvenience, for themselves and for their users, for two reasons.

1. To spam you
2. To sell your data

Selling means a [data management platform](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_management_platform) (DMP). I have, unfortunately, built them. An email is how you are joined to the cookies of advertising networks. Registering with one is volunteering to be marked, across the web, as a cohort interested in the service the site provides.

## It should be illegal

Requiring an email to register is one of the very few things I hope to see regulated. A service that asks for one is blackmailing you: consent to be spammed and to have your data sold, or be refused the service. That should be illegal. Every service should be obliged to offer an anonymous alternative.

